// Connections in wdoc diagrams.
//
// Shapes are identified by their `id` field. Connection statements
// reference those ids — they may sit anywhere within the diagram
// (including deep inside a container) and the renderer draws an
// arrow between the resolved shapes. The bundled `Edge` schema
// (declared in wdoc.wcl) accepts any pair of SvgBlocks; users may
// declare their own connection schemas and consume them via
// `@connections(MyEdge)` on a custom block type.

page connections { sites = [:showcase]
  h1 "Connections"

  text {
    span "Shapes connect via `lhs -> rhs :kind` statements. The renderer ranks shapes via the layered solver, routes elbow paths around other shapes via A*, and nudges parallel segments apart so dense graphs stay readable."
  }

  h2 "Flat connections"

  // Manual x/y on each shape — useful when you want pixel-exact
  // placement. Edges are still routed by the elbow A* router.
  diagram {
    width     = 360
    height    = 140
    layout    = :layered
    direction = :left_to_right
    layer_gap = 30.0

    process    "Validate" { id = step_a width = 80.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#eef" stroke = "#446" }
    decision   "Match?"   { id = step_b width = 80.0 height = 60.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
    terminator "Done"     { id = step_c width = 70.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#efe" stroke = "#494" }

    step_a -> step_b
    step_b -> step_c :flow
  }

  h2 "Branching flow"

  // Wide DAG: one entry fans out into two parallel branches that
  // each visit a couple of stops before reconverging on a single
  // merge decision and final terminator.
  diagram {
    width     = 520
    height    = 360
    layout    = :layered
    layer_gap = 30.0

    process    "Start"      { id = start    width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#eef" stroke = "#446" }
    decision   "Route?"     { id = branch   width = 100.0 height = 60.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
    process    "Path A.1"   { id = path_a1  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    process    "Path A.2"   { id = path_a2  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    process    "Path B.1"   { id = path_b1  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#ecc" stroke = "#744" }
    process    "Path B.2"   { id = path_b2  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#ecc" stroke = "#744" }
    decision   "Merge OK?"  { id = merge    width = 100.0 height = 60.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
    terminator "Done"       { id = done     width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#efe" stroke = "#494" }

    start  -> branch  :flow
    branch -> path_a1 :flow
    branch -> path_b1 :flow
    path_a1 -> path_a2 :flow
    path_b1 -> path_b2 :flow
    path_a2 -> merge  :flow
    path_b2 -> merge  :flow
    merge   -> done   :flow
  }

  h2 "Force-directed layout"

  text {
    span "`layout = :force` runs a force-directed simulation instead of ranking shapes: every shape repels every other, while `@connections(Edge)` edges act as springs pulling connected shapes together. Nodes start clustered in the centre and settle into an organic spread that surfaces clusters — ideal for cyclic or undirected graphs with no natural rank order. The layout is deterministic for a given `seed`; tune the spread with `repulsion`, `link_distance`, and `gravity`. Straight `routing` suits the radial edges. Here the nodes are labelled `node` circles, colour-coded by role (red core, blue front-end, green workers, amber data)."
  }

  diagram {
    width         = 460
    height        = 360
    layout        = :force
    routing       = :straight
    link_distance = 70.0

    node "Core"    { id = mesh_core    width = 64.0 height = 64.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
    node "Auth"    { id = mesh_auth    width = 60.0 height = 60.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    node "API"     { id = mesh_api     width = 56.0 height = 56.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    node "Cache"   { id = mesh_cache   width = 62.0 height = 62.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    node "Worker"  { id = mesh_worker  width = 70.0 height = 70.0 fill = "#efe" stroke = "#494" }
    node "Queue"   { id = mesh_queue   width = 62.0 height = 62.0 fill = "#efe" stroke = "#494" }
    node "DB"      { id = mesh_db      width = 56.0 height = 56.0 fill = "#fec" stroke = "#a74" }
    node "Metrics" { id = mesh_metrics width = 72.0 height = 72.0 fill = "#fec" stroke = "#a74" }

    // A cyclic service mesh — no rank order, so :layered would force an
    // arbitrary one. The spring/repulsion balance lets the clusters
    // (front-end blue, workers green, data amber) find their own room.
    mesh_core   -> mesh_auth
    mesh_core   -> mesh_api
    mesh_core   -> mesh_metrics
    mesh_api    -> mesh_cache
    mesh_api    -> mesh_db
    mesh_api    -> mesh_queue
    mesh_queue  -> mesh_worker
    mesh_worker -> mesh_db
    mesh_auth   -> mesh_db
  }

  h2 "Sub-graphs in containers"

  text {
    span "Each container runs its own layered solver via its `@connections(Edge) edges` field. The diagram-level `norm -> validate` cross-container edge bridges them."
  }

  diagram {
    width     = 600
    height    = 280
    layout    = :layered
    direction = :left_to_right
    layer_gap = 60.0

    container {
      id        = ingest
      width     = 240.0
      height    = 240.0
      layout    = :layered
      layer_gap = 20.0

      process "Fetch"     { id = fetch width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
      process "Parse"     { id = parse width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
      process "Normalize" { id = norm  width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }

      fetch -> parse :flow
      parse -> norm  :flow
    }

    container {
      id        = pipeline
      width     = 240.0
      height    = 240.0
      layout    = :layered
      layer_gap = 20.0

      decision   "Validate?" { id = validate width = 120.0 height = 60.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
      process    "Index"     { id = index    width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#ecc" stroke = "#744" }
      terminator "Persist"   { id = persist  width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#efe" stroke = "#494" }

      validate -> index   :flow
      index    -> persist :flow
    }

    // Cross-container bridge. The recursive id resolver finds
    // `norm` inside `ingest` and `validate` inside `pipeline`.
    norm -> validate :data
  }

  h2 "Dense cross-container graph"

  text {
    span "Three labelled containers form a 3-tier topology. Each container runs its own layered solver internally and auto-fits to its contents (`width` / `height` omitted), so the visible chrome (`stroke = ...`) hugs the shapes inside. The outer diagram uses a 3-cell grid so the containers sit side-by-side. Seven cross-container edges connect shapes across tiers, including a pair sharing `orders` as their source so the edge-separation pass has something to nudge apart. Container chrome is purely visual and does not participate in obstacle routing — arrows cross container borders cleanly. This diagram sets `pan_zoom = true`, so you can scroll to zoom, drag to pan, and use the corner +/−/⟲ controls."
  }

  diagram {
    width       = 720
    height      = 320
    layout      = :grid
    columns     = 3
    cell_width  = 200.0
    cell_height = 320.0
    gap         = 40.0

    // Interactive: scroll to zoom, drag to pan, or use the corner
    // controls. Zoom is capped between the fitted view and 4x.
    pan_zoom = true
    zoom_min = 1.0
    zoom_max = 4.0

    container {
      id        = frontend
      stroke    = "#888"
      padding   = 12.0
      layout    = :layered
      layer_gap = 16.0

      process "Login"    { id = login    width = 120.0 height = 36.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
      process "Browse"   { id = browse   width = 120.0 height = 36.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
      process "Cart"     { id = cart     width = 120.0 height = 36.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
      process "Checkout" { id = checkout width = 120.0 height = 36.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }

      // Chain ranks the shapes top-to-bottom; matches a realistic
      // user flow through the storefront.
      login  -> browse   :flow
      browse -> cart     :flow
      cart   -> checkout :flow
    }

    container {
      id        = services
      stroke    = "#888"
      padding   = 12.0
      layout    = :layered
      layer_gap = 16.0

      decision "Auth"    { id = auth    width = 120.0 height = 50.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
      process  "Catalog" { id = catalog width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#fec" stroke = "#a74" }
      process  "Orders"  { id = orders  width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#fec" stroke = "#a74" }

      auth    -> catalog :flow
      catalog -> orders  :flow
    }

    container {
      id        = storage
      stroke    = "#888"
      padding   = 12.0
      layout    = :layered
      layer_gap = 16.0

      terminator "Users DB"    { id = users_db    width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cec" stroke = "#494" }
      terminator "Products DB" { id = products_db width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cec" stroke = "#494" }
      terminator "Orders DB"   { id = orders_db   width = 120.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cec" stroke = "#494" }

      // No natural data dependency between the three DBs; chain
      // them just so the layered solver stacks them vertically
      // instead of placing them all at rank 0 side-by-side.
      users_db    -> products_db :flow
      products_db -> orders_db   :flow
    }

    // Cross-container edges. Frontend → services.
    login    -> auth    :flow
    browse   -> catalog :flow
    checkout -> orders  :flow

    // Services → storage. Orders writes to both DBs to exercise
    // the parallel-edge separation pass.
    auth    -> users_db    :data
    catalog -> products_db :data
    orders  -> orders_db   :data
    orders  -> users_db    :data
  }

  h2 "Obstacle routing"

  // Three rects laid out left-to-right by the layered solver. The
  // middle node is part of the chain so the A* router still has to
  // bend the path around it when src and dst share a y.
  diagram {
    width     = 360
    height    = 120
    layout    = :layered
    direction = :left_to_right
    layer_gap = 30.0

    rect { id = src_a   width = 60.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#446" }
    rect { id = blocker width = 60.0 height = 80.0 fill = "#ddd" stroke = "#888" }
    rect { id = dst_a   width = 60.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cec" stroke = "#494" }

    src_a   -> blocker :flow
    blocker -> dst_a   :flow
  }

  h2 "Hub topology"

  text {
    span "One central node receives from three sources and feeds three sinks. The edge-separation pass keeps the six surrounding corridors visible."
  }

  diagram {
    width     = 520
    height    = 280
    layout    = :layered
    layer_gap = 30.0

    process "Source 1" { id = src1  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    process "Source 2" { id = src2  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    process "Source 3" { id = src3  width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#cce" stroke = "#447" }
    process "Hub"      { id = hub   width = 120.0 height = 50.0 fill = "#fee" stroke = "#a44" }
    process "Sink 1"   { id = sink1 width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#ecc" stroke = "#744" }
    process "Sink 2"   { id = sink2 width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#ecc" stroke = "#744" }
    process "Sink 3"   { id = sink3 width = 100.0 height = 40.0 fill = "#ecc" stroke = "#744" }

    src1 -> hub :flow
    src2 -> hub :flow
    src3 -> hub :flow
    hub  -> sink1 :flow
    hub  -> sink2 :flow
    hub  -> sink3 :flow
  }

  h2 "Restricted anchors"

  text {
    span "`connect_points` narrows where an edge may attach. The middle node only accepts arrivals on its `:south` side, so the second arrow has to bend down then over."
  }

  diagram {
    width     = 360
    height    = 160
    layout    = :layered
    direction = :left_to_right
    layer_gap = 40.0

    rect {
      id = sender
      width = 80.0  height = 40.0
      fill = "#cce"  stroke = "#447"
      connect_points = [:east]
    }
    rect {
      id = waystation
      width = 80.0  height = 40.0
      fill = "#fee"  stroke = "#a44"
      connect_points = [:south]
    }
    rect {
      id = receiver
      width = 80.0  height = 40.0
      fill = "#cec"  stroke = "#494"
      connect_points = [:west]
    }

    sender     -> waystation :data
    waystation -> receiver   :data
  }

  text {
    span "Back to [the overview](overview) or read [About](about)."
  }
}
